WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.218 --> 00:00:02.760
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: If you are
ingesting large amounts of data

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.890
for analysis or
looking to simplify

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.800
the development of event
driven microservices,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.832
then you need to check
out Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:00:10.832 --> 00:00:13.262
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.720
Welcome to Pub/Sub Made Easy.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.430
I'm Priyanka.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:23.690
And in today's episode, we will
dive deeper into Subscribers.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.110
A publisher sent messages
to a Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.140
Subscriber application creates
a subscription to this topic

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.659
in order to receive
those messages.

00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:35.360
A topic can have multiple
subscriptions but a given

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.970
subscription belongs
to a single topic,

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.030
which means a subscription
cannot be receiving messages

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.220
from more than one topics.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.930
Check out episode
2 of the series

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.120
to see how to set
up subscriptions.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.230
Cloud Pub/Sub delivers each
published message at least once

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.067
for every subscription.

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.400
There are some exceptions to
this at least once behavior

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.150
though.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.730
You can configure message
retention duration.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.240
And the value can be between
10 minutes and seven days.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.450
By default, a message that
cannot be delivered within

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.560
the maximum retention time of
seven days is deleted and is no

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.940
longer accessible.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.330
This typically happens when
a subscriber does not keep up

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.230
with the flow of the messages.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.220
Oh, and make sure to create
at least one subscription

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.480
when you create a topic.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.160
Otherwise, the
messages published

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.020
to the topic with
no subscription

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.060
disappear into the ether.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.730
Once a message is
sent to a subscriber,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.130
the subscriber should
acknowledge the message.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:34.910
But until it does, the message
is considered outstanding.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.790
And it stays outstanding
until the subscriber

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.820
reaches the configurable
limited amount of time

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.550
called ACT deadline,
at which point

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.430
Cloud Pub/Sub will
attempt to redeliver it.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.490
And while the message is
outstanding to a subscriber,

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.740
Cloud Pub/Sub tries
not to deliver it

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.490
to any other subscriber
on the same subscription.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:59.180
Typically, Cloud Pub/Sub
delivers each message once

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.470
in the order in which
it was published.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.430
However, messages may
sometimes be delivered out

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.440
of order or more than once.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.650
Idempotency and duplication
is important to think

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.980
about regardless of Pub/Sub
since it can happen simply

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.920
because publishers
republish the same content.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.310
A very simple way to achieve
exactly once processing

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:23.270
is to rely on Cloud Dataflow's
Pub/Sub I/O connector which

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.820
solves some of the harder
problems like this for you.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.270
You can also achieve
ordered processing

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.460
by having the publishers
send the sequence

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.960
token in the message.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.050
Stay tuned for more on this
in the upcoming episodes.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.470
Alternatively, Cloud Dataflow
gives you very simple

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:42.680
to use tools to produce
ordered output or process

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.760
messages in order.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.010
Now a subscription can use
either pull or push mechanism

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:50.430
for message delivery.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.810
And it's configurable.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.590
So you can change or configure
the mechanism at any time.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.670
In pull delivery, the
subscribing application

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.100
explicitly calls the
pull method which

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.590
requests messages for delivery.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.570
The Cloud Pub/Sub
server responds

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.850
with a message or an
error if the queue

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.250
is empty and an Ack ID.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.730
The subscriber explicitly
calls the acknowledged method

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:17.330
using the returned Ack ID
to acknowledge receipt.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.060
A lot of this is abstracted
in the client libraries

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.630
where you might simply create
callbacks that are invoked

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.550
when a new message arrives.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.620
The client library
itself will manage

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.920
all the acknowledgment
deadlines and extensions.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.690
In push delivery, the
Cloud Pub/Sub servers

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.640
sends each message
as an HTTPS request

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.760
to the subscriber application
at a pre-configured endpoint.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.410
The endpoint
acknowledges the message

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.110
by returning an HTTP
success status code.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.470
A nonsuccess response
indicates that the message

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:49.880
should be resent.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.250
Cloud Pub/Sub dynamically
adjusts the rate

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.650
of push requests based
on the rate at which it

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.030
receives success responses.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.390
Now that we know how push
and pull subscriptions work,

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:02.030
how can we choose
which method should

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.920
be best for our use case?

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.100
Well, stay tuned for the
next episode to find out.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.100
Now let's talk about the
lifecycle of subscription.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.150
By default, subscription expires
after 31 days of inactivity.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.640
Inactivity can be no
active connections,

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.310
pull requests, or
push successes.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:21.829
If Cloud Pub/Sub detects
subscriber activity,

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.530
the subscription
deletion clock restarts.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.290
But by using subscription
expiration policies,

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.050
you can configure the
inactivity duration

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.000
or make the
subscription persistent

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.990
regardless of activity.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.770
You can also delete a
subscription manually.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.680
And if you create a
subscription with the same name

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.870
as the deleted one,
the new subscription

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.240
has no relationship
with the old one.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.940
Today be learned how to
subscribe to Cloud Pub/Sub

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:47.840
messages.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.490
We looked at least once
delivery, push, and pull

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.230
methods.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:54.560
Join us next time to learn more
about choosing between push

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.000
and pull subscribers.

